# XSH setlocale()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Only what the text fixes is asserted: the POSIX locale and its two names, the
# query form, the all-or-nothing behaviour of a failed call, the effect of the
# environment on setlocale(LC_ALL, ""), and what a returned name can be used for.
# The text of any name other than "C" and "POSIX" is implementation-defined, so a
# name returned for another locale is never compared with a fixed string; tests
# that need a second locale take one from pc_find_locale("UTF-8", 0) and are
# NOTINUSE when the system has none. A locale name that is certainly not installed
# is made up for the failure tests; if the system accepts it the test is
# UNRESOLVED. The tests change the environment of their own process only.

assertion  XSH.setlocale.001
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap07.html#7.2
class      required
says       Before any call to setlocale(), the global locale is the POSIX
           locale: the langinfo items of the time, numeric and messages
           categories have the values XBD 7.3.5.3, 7.3.4.1 and 7.3.6.1 give,
           localeconv() shows the POSIX numeric and monetary values, strcoll()
           orders "B" before "a", and a query of LC_ALL and of each category
           returns a non-null pointer.
test       t/001-initial.c

assertion  XSH.setlocale.002
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For LC_ALL and for each of the six categories, both "C" and "POSIX"
           are accepted (non-null return), and afterwards that part of the
           global locale behaves as the POSIX locale, also when a different
           locale had been selected for it before.
test       t/002-names.c

assertion  XSH.setlocale.003
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       A query with a null locale argument returns a non-null string for
           LC_ALL and for each category, does not change the global locale,
           and two consecutive queries of the same category give strings with
           the same content. (The state queried is a mixed one when a second
           locale is installed, else the POSIX locale.)
test       t/003-query.c

assertion  XSH.setlocale.004
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       Setting one category to another locale leaves the other five
           categories as they were (their queried names are unchanged and
           they still behave as the POSIX locale), the string returned by the
           call is accepted again by setlocale() for that category, and the
           langinfo code set follows LC_CTYPE only.
test       t/004-one-category.c

assertion  XSH.setlocale.005
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       The string a query of LC_ALL returns, passed back with LC_ALL,
           restores every category as it was (a mixed state with LC_CTYPE and
           LC_TIME from another locale), and the string a query of one category
           returns, passed back with that category, restores that part.
test       t/005-restore.c

assertion  XSH.setlocale.006
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       A call with a name that no locale has, for LC_ALL and for one
           category, returns a null pointer and leaves every category of the
           global locale as it was (names unchanged, behaviour unchanged).
test       t/006-failure.c

assertion  XSH.setlocale.007
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap08.html#8.2
class      required
says       setlocale(LC_ALL, "") succeeds and selects the POSIX locale for
           every category when the environment names it through: LC_ALL (set to
           "POSIX", with LC_* and LANG naming a missing locale, which shows LC_ALL
           takes precedence); the six LC_* variables each set to "C" with LANG
           naming a missing locale; LANG alone set to "POSIX".
test       t/007-env-success.c

assertion  XSH.setlocale.008
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       setlocale(LC_ALL, "") returns a null pointer and changes nothing
           (no category, not even one whose variable names a good locale) when
           the variable that applies to one category names a missing locale:
           LC_TIME naming it with LANG good; and LC_ALL naming it with the
           LC_* variables good.
test       t/008-env-failure.c

assertion  XSH.setlocale.009
anchor     functions/setlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/uselocale.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       While a thread has a thread-local locale installed with uselocale(),
           a change of the global locale with setlocale() does not affect that
           thread (a POSIX-locale object stays POSIX); once the thread
           reinstalls LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE it sees the global locale as changed.
test       t/009-thread-locale.c
